<h1>Present a deck from anywhere</h1>

<p>Paste a link to a deck — a <strong>GitHub gist</strong> (best: the API
allows browser fetches), a raw markdown URL, or a JSON manifest URL —
and present it right here using deckhand's WebAssembly build. Gists are
resolved exactly like the CLI does it: <code>deck.json</code> first, then
<code>deck.md</code>, then the only file, then the first <code>.md</code>.</p>

<form class="play-form" id="play-form">
  <input type="url" id="play-url" required
         placeholder="https://gist.github.com/you/abc123"
         autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">
  <button type="submit">present</button>
</form>
<p id="play-status"></p>

<div class="terminal-frame" id="play-terminal"></div>
<div class="terminal-caption" id="play-caption"></div>
<p class="hint-bar"><kbd>space</kbd> next · <kbd>shift-space</kbd> back ·
<kbd>o</kbd> overview · <kbd>↓</kbd>/<kbd>↑</kbd> depth — share this page
with <code>?deck=&lt;url&gt;</code> to deep-link a deck</p>

<h2>Notes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Terminal blocks show as placeholders here — browsers can't spawn
  PTYs. To run them for real: <code>deckhand &lt;the same url&gt;</code>.
  If the deck was compiled with <code>deckhand compile --snapshots</code>,
  you'll see a real capture of each command's output instead.</li>
  <li>Plain URLs need CORS headers to be fetchable from a browser;
  gists and <code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code> links work out of the
  box.</li>
  <li>A gist can hold a whole multi-file deck: a <code>deck.json</code>
  manifest plus slide files, resolved by filename.</li>
</ul>
